            <ArticleTitle>Isolated Renal Hydatidosis Presenting as Renal Mass: A Diagnostic Dilemma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>103-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.eucr.2015.04.002</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hydatid disease is a parasitic infestation by larval form of Echinococcus granulosus. Isolated renal involvement is extremely rare. There are no specific signs and symptoms of renal hydatidosis. However it may present as palpable mass, flank pain, hematuria, malaise, fever, and hydatiduria or as a complication of it such as infection, abscess, hemorrhage, necrosis and pelviureteric junction obstruction, renal failure etc. Except hydatiduria, none are pathognomonic for renal hydatidosis. There is no literature on renal hydatidosis presenting as renal mass we report 2 cases of isolated renal hydatidosis, which mimicked a renal mass on imaging study.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
